/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Final_checklist.md:367:Five runs is enough to prove determinism here because nothing in the tests-only channel is stochastic: a disagreement among the five is a flaky check, never noise from a model.
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Final_checklist.md:369:The owner-only run exists because the oracle lies about file modes. `solve.sh` installs its program with `cp`, which leaves 0644; a real agent writes the same program through its editor, which creates a temp file and renames it, so the file arrives at 0600, owned by uid 1000 and readable by nobody else. Harbor hands `/app` to the verifier with those modes intact. A verifier that runs the program under `setpriv --reuid=nobody` (or Popen `user=`) then cannot open it, every check that executes the program fails, and the ordinary oracle run still reads 1.000, so the bundle ships and every real trajectory loses that whole tier. The owner-only copy reproduces the agent's mode and must read exactly 1.000 like the other five. Anything lower is a verifier defect, not an agent one: the fix is to chmod the program and its parent directories inside the verifier (it runs as root) or to execute a copy the verifier staged itself, never to relax the privilege drop. tests_qc H6 reads the same property statically; this run is the proof (TS-30).
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Final_checklist.md:371:`-y` is not optional. The `[verifier.env]` block makes Harbor stop and ask "Tasks in this run will load these from your environment. Proceed?", and without `-y` the command sits waiting for input and then aborts. Harbor also prints "The verifier.env contains an API key ... You will incur costs associated with the API calls" on every run. That line is expected and is not a finding: the copy has no judge, so nothing is spent even when the reviewer's environment exports a key.
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Final_checklist.md:382:That is the host, not the bundle. Harbor checks whether the Docker daemon's kernel can enforce the policy (it probes for `CONFIG_NFT_FIB_INET`); Docker Desktop's VM kernel does not have it, so Harbor declines any policy it cannot enforce, and `allowlist` is rejected for exactly the same reason `no-network` is. `[agent]` must move with the others: Harbor checks the agent-phase policy before the run starts, whatever the agent is, so an `[agent]` allowlist over a public `[environment]` still refuses to start, even for oracle and nop. Opening the network changes no score: the oracle does the work offline from the shipped data, nop does nothing, the stub only touches the declared paths, and the copy has no judge to reach. Keep the two claims separate in the sign-off: the scores are evidence about grading, not evidence that the declared offline policy is enforceable. The policy itself stays a read-only check (FC-S12, IN-11, DS-11, AL-15).
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Final_checklist.md:384:Real agent runs are not Step 10. They grade both channels on the shipped bundle and must pass the judge key, e.g. `--ve OPENAI_API_KEY=$OPENAI_API_KEY`; without it the verifier errors by design, because a run whose judged half was never scored has no reward to report.
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Final_checklist.md:386:Never edit the bundle under review. Change the copies only, and delete both copies when the runs are done. A `task.toml` that says `"public"`, a missing `judge.toml`, or a tests-only `reward.toml` in the bundle after the review is a worse defect than the one you were working around.
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Final_checklist.md:390:- Every one of the five oracle runs must read exactly `1.000`. Report all five numbers, not a summary. Four ones and a `0.981` is a fail, and it is a more serious fail than a flat `0.9`, because the verifier is not measuring the submission: the same bytes scored two different ways. Reward that moves on its own is noise the agent is punished for and cannot control, and because reward here is weighted rather than pass/fail, one flaky test worth 3 of 53 points drifts the number without ever flipping a verdict. When the five disagree, find the cause (gate 03 TS-11 lists where to look) rather than re-running until five agree.
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Final_checklist.md:391:- nop reward must be exactly `0.000`. nop does no work at all, so any positive score means some test passes on the untouched container (free reward, an F0c work-dependency break), and any negative score means a cheat probe fires without any cheat present (a broken negative test an honest agent could also trip).

